<abstractGroup>
<abstract>
<p>A new iridium-catalyzed method enables the <italic>enantioselective</italic> synthesis of chiral amines with up to 99% ee.</p>
</abstract>
</abstractGroup>
<section>
<title>Introduction</title>
<p>Chiral amines are ubiquitous in pharmaceuticals and agrochemicals. Despite advances in asymmetric catalysis, many transformations still require <bold>stoichiometric</bold> chiral auxiliaries.</p>
</section>
<section>
<title>Results and Discussion</title>
<section>
<title>Catalyst Optimization</title>
<p>We screened a library of 24 phosphine-oxazoline ligands for the iridium-catalyzed amination.</p>
</section>
<section>
<title>Substrate Scope</title>
<p>The optimized catalyst system tolerated a range of functional groups:</p>
<tabular>
<label>Table 1</label>
<title>Substrate scope for the asymmetric amination</title>
<table>
<tgroup cols="3">
<thead>
<row><entry>Substrate</entry><entry>Yield (%)</entry><entry>ee (%)</entry></row>
</thead>
<tbody>
<row><entry>Aryl ketone</entry><entry>95</entry><entry>99</entry></row>
<row><entry>Alkyl ketone</entry><entry>82</entry><entry>94</entry></row>
<row><entry>Cyclic imine</entry><entry>91</entry><entry>97</entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</table>
</tabular>
</section>
